phases.summary:
Read-only map of the split protocol at d4d631df. No files were edited and no cargo was run. There are four main findings.

(1) A split has only three durable steps:
- Phase A writes the intent: a registry CAS sets map.pending.
- The parent's segment identity is sealed by an empty close op sent to its committer.
- Phase B publishes the children and clears the intent in one CAS, then retires the scaler sketch and invalidates the registry.

A split has no engine-handle retirement and no old-owner engine close. The low child keeps the parent's route, so it stays on the same engine. The high child gets a new route, salted until it lands on a different shard prefix, and that engine opens lazily the first time a request resolves it. An "old owner" exists only across instances: the seal is relayed through /v1/internal/segment-close, and appends that reach the wrong instance get a 409 NotOwner redirect.

(2) Appends choose their segment with resolve_segment, which never looks at map.pending. So through the whole window from Phase A to Phase B, appends go to the parent.
- If an append is staged before the close op, it commits normally, with an offset below the frozen offset.
- If it is staged after the close, it gets AppendErr::Closed. closure_is_current then refreshes the descriptor. If a transition is pending, it schedules a request_work resume and waits for it (at most 10 s). The append is retried up to 4 times, with waits of 10, 20 and 30 ms. It either lands on a child or gets 503 segment_transition with Retry-After 1.
- A stale cached descriptor after publication takes the same Closed → refresh → retry path.

(3) In post_split_throughput_scales, no append is in flight during execute_split. blast_keys joins all 48 client tasks before it returns. execute_split is awaited. The test then calls engine_for_scaler (Adoption::Internal), which opens both child engines, and only then starts the warm-up. So the 500 went to an append issued after Phase B published and after the registry was invalidated, with both child engines already open. None of the split-boundary windows the reviewer lists had traffic in that run. The only new things at that point were:
- first appends to new child identities (tail handle load, billing-row load);
- a freshly opened high-child engine, with maintenance rebuilt and an absorber running at 1-byte / 1-ms thresholds;
- the parent's closed identity being absorbed and trimmed on the low child's engine.

The HTTP rigs run only the http and request-maintenance tasks. They start no autonomous scaler (scaler3::start is called only from bootstrap.rs:761), no billing sweep and no fleet loop, so nothing closes or fences an engine before engine_shutdown at the end of the test. Item 47 needs a closed or fenced store, so it has no trigger in that rig.

The product surface turns every Internal-class failure (Internal and ShardOpen) into a bare "500 append_failed / append failed", and blast_client asserts only the status and throws the body away. The 500's cause was therefore never recorded. The committer's write and handle-load failures are not logged either, while accounting divergence and open failures are. A handler panic does not produce a 500: it closes the socket, the client sees None, and it retries.

(4) Pause mechanisms that exist today:
- the per-name failpoints registry: ScalerBeforePublish, and AppendBeforeEnqueue;
- CloseBeforeEnqueue exists but does not cover the split's seal, because the seal calls try_close directly;
- engine hooks: test_hold_commit, test_hold_dispatch, fail_next_group_for, appends_enqueued;
- FaultStore.hold_class, which is store-wide and keyed by operation and object class;
- registry hooks: test_poison_cache, fail_next_get, fail_next_put;
- the shard directory's GatePark and the rig's open_park;
- request_work test_admit with Action::Held;
- the engine's completion_pause at NoWrite / Written / Durable, which is private to shard::retirement_tests.

Hooks that are missing:
- a pause between the Phase A CAS and the seal enqueue (topology.rs just before line 428);
- a pause after the Phase B CAS and before invalidation (topology.rs:540);
- a per-identity pause on a child's first handle or billing load.

No existing DST test sends concurrent appends across the seal boundary and records each outcome.

items.detail:
In post_split_throughput_scales the store is FaultStore::uniform with FaultPlan{latency 100% at 20 ms, error 0, lost 0}, so injected store errors are ruled out. The rig starts per-engine absorbers at threshold_bytes 1 / threshold_age 1 ms (fixture_http.rs:306-312), so Absorbed ops share commit groups with appends constantly, on both the parent/low engine and the new high engine. A group-level rejection answers every append in that group.
Remaining candidates for a one-off post-split 500:
- accounting divergence: look for its tracing::error;
- a SlateDB get or write error on a child identity's first use;
- OpenFailed on a reopen: nothing in the rig closes engines, so this is unlikely.
Item 47 needs an engine close or fence, which this rig never performs mid-run.

All of these either split with no concurrent appends, or park at ScalerBeforePublish and then exercise reads, livefeed connects, seals or single stale-descriptor appends. None runs concurrent appends through the seal itself (append enqueued before vs after the parent close in one held group), through the child's first open or handle load, or across an engine close during a held group write, and none records each append's committed / rejected / ambiguous outcome.
The building blocks exist:
- test_hold_commit + appends_enqueued for the before/after-close ordering;
- park_append_before_enqueue for an append already routed to the parent;
- rig_in_seal_gap for the gap;
- test_poison_cache for stale routing;
- GatePark or open_park for the child engine open;
- hold_class(Put, Wal) + shards.retire for item 47.
Still missing: a pre-seal failpoint (topology.rs:427) and a post-publish failpoint (topology.rs:540).
